Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Reliquary**, a tall, precious monument that cradles a
glowing solar core, encircled by a turning orbital ring and crowned by counter-rotating
guardian fins — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You
build it by **painting discrete opaque cells** and you **author its ring, core, and fin
animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Reliquary is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you
break the monument into a fixed body and its moving pieces, where you place the joints,
and how you shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a
self-cycling reliquary needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` —
together with the rig you define are your submission.

You paint the geometry as **discrete opaque cells**: `voxel-anim` sets and clears single
voxels, fills and strokes boxes, draws 3D lines and spheres, and mirrors across a plane
— each cell an opaque `#rrggbb` color; there is **no transparency**. Every voxel
operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted **separately**
in its own preview and log, in the shared volume's coordinates. The volume starts
**empty**.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a part
with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it. Each call **records
only** — it appends the operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run
`voxel-anim render` when you want to (re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`)
and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name plinth
voxel-anim fill-box --part plinth --x0 1 --y0 0 --z0 1 --x1 5 --y1 5 --z1 5 --color "#c69a4b"
voxel-anim add-sphere --part solar_core --cx 3 --cy 3 --cz 3 --r 2 --color "#ff9d2e"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every voxel operation **and** the rig and animation
subcommands (defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations
with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Run
`voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's progress **and
the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole monument composed from all your parts,
drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the core sits cradled at the monument's heart, the ring
encircles it, and the fins crown it. Because each part is sculpted in the shared
volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the finished monument; the
scene simply composes them there.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build and rig the reliquary up operation by operation until each `parts/<part>.png`
matches the brief, the assembled `scene/` previews show the parts fitting together
correctly, and the rig is complete: a tall masonry plinth and cradle, a glowing solar
core at its heart, an orbital ring encircling it, and guardian fins crowning it.
**Author the three required animations** as F-curves with the `voxel-anim` animation
subcommands: `ring_spin` (a smooth, steady full revolution of the ring), `core_pulse`
(a slow breathing rise-and-settle of the core, eased so the breath carries weight), and
`fins_spin` (a full revolution of the fins the OPPOSITE way from the ring, so they
visibly counter-rotate) — all three self-playing, so the monument cycles on its own
while the plinth stays fixed. The animations are the fixed contract — produce all three,
by those names; the parts and joints that realize them are yours. Before you finish, run `voxel-anim render` so it emits the
per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part is scored as
empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` redraws one part; `voxel-anim render
--time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant of an animation
to check the motion). When you are
satisfied, stop: the recorded per-part logs and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
